description:
crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:99 derives PartialEq on Sha256, which (via the inner String) inherits the standard variable-time comparison: short-circuit on first byte mismatch. This is the correct default for a wire-format type — there is no in-scope use case in the JMAP CID draft where Sha256 comparison is on the timing-attack path.

But the contract is undocumented. A downstream consumer reading the type-level rustdoc has no signal whether == on Sha256 is timing-safe. A blob-integrity check inside an authenticated flow — e.g. \"compare the digest the client claimed against the digest the server computed\" — is not a real timing-attack vector (both digests are public to the user with the blob). But a consumer who repurposes Sha256 as a token-equality type or a content-addressable-storage hash compared against attacker-controlled input could leak digest prefixes through timing.

The fix is a docstring note, not an implementation change. Suggested addition to the Sha256 type rustdoc:

    /// # Equality
    ///
    /// PartialEq on Sha256 is the standard variable-time String compare
    /// — short-circuits on first byte mismatch. This is appropriate for
    /// blob integrity and content-addressed lookups (both sides have
    /// public access to the digest), and inappropriate for comparing
    /// against attacker-controlled input where timing could leak digest
    /// prefixes. For the latter, use a constant-time comparison crate
    /// (e.g. subtle::ConstantTimeEq) on Sha256::as_str().

This is api-contract,docs because the contract IS the variable-time behavior; surfacing it in rustdoc prevents downstream misuse without changing the public surface. The same critique applies to Id, UTCDate, State in jmap-types (all derive PartialEq on String) — those are out of scope here but a workspace-wide canonical sweep could carry the same note.
